= The Third Reformist Conference

The invitations go out in the spring: the Third Reformist Conference, to convene at Cainholde, in the <span style="color: #750e0e;">**Central Union**</span>. Every four years the Reformist parties of the continent meet to argue out what the movement is for — wages and hours and the franchise, the tariff question, what a party of labor owes a state it does not control. That the <span style="color: #750e0e;">Central Union</span> is willing to host the thing at all is the first interesting fact about it, and the delegations have noticed.

Our own attendance was never in question. The question, as it was at the Second Conference, is whether the <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> will come.

They came to the First. By the Second they had already begun the long walk toward the position they hold now — that reformism is not an ally to be argued with but a fraud to be exposed, and that the Marchers are the more dangerous half of the fraud precisely because workers still believe us. Since then the delegations have not shared a room.

But the crash has changed the arithmetic for everyone. Across the continent, parties that spent the twenties certain of their own analysis are discovering how little of it survived contact with mass unemployment. There is a hope — thin, but real — that the <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> might use Cainholde to climb down.

[? if collectivist_leader == "Conciliators": Under the Conciliators, they do not need to be asked. The delegation is named within the week, and it is a serious one — not the usual pair of observers sent to file a denunciation, but the men who actually run the party's unions. ?][? if collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 3): Halsmark has not said no. That in itself is news. The party press has run nothing on Cainholde in either direction for three weeks, which among the <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> is how an argument sounds from the outside. Someone could push. ?][? if collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and not (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 3): The reply comes back within days, and it is not a refusal so much as a lecture. The <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> will not attend. To sit at Cainholde would be to concede that the reformist movement is a wing of the workers' cause rather than a betrayal of it, and they decline to concede anything of the kind — least of all to us. ?]

