@conciliators_attend
title: Then we go to Cainholde together.
view-if: collectivist_leader == "Conciliators"
on-arrival: left_strength += 3; left_dissent -= 4 if left_dissent >= 4; center_dissent -= 3 if center_dissent >= 3; collectivist_relation += 8*(1-dissent); commons_marcher += 2; disfavored_marcher += 2; soviet_relation += 1; west_relation += 1

For the first time since the First Conference, the two delegations travel as delegations rather than as rivals with the same destination. The resolutions that come out of Cainholde are not remarkable — they rarely are — but the photograph is: our people and theirs on the same platform, and no one storming out of it.

The effect at home is felt within the month. The <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> quietly retire the sharper end of their anti-Marcher line — the social-fascism language, the leaflets naming our ministers as the workers' real enemy — and in the factory districts and the labor exchanges that language had been doing more damage than either party admitted. Our own <span style="color: #c00000;">Left</span> is vindicated and says so; the argument it has been losing for six years is suddenly the argument that was right.

Abroad, the reaction is warmer still. The <span style="color: #750e0e;">**Central Union**</span> has spent two years insisting that its parties abroad were not isolating themselves, and Cainholde lets it prove the point on its own soil. The <span style="color: #D70926;">Nievalian</span> Reformists, who had watched the northern kingdoms' own collectivists follow the hard line into irrelevance, are frankly relieved.

@pay
title: Send an approach through the unions.
subtitle: -1 resource. The industrial federations still talk to each other even when the parties do not.
view-if: collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 3)
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: Not enough resources (1 needed).
on-arrival: resources -= 1; left_strength += 3; left_dissent -= 4 if left_dissent >= 4; center_dissent -= 3 if center_dissent >= 3; collectivist_relation += 8*(1-dissent); commons_marcher += 2; disfavored_marcher += 2

We do not approach the party. We approach the men in the industrial federations who have to share a shop floor with our members whatever the party press says, and we let them make the case upward — that a delegation costs nothing, that refusing looks like fear, that the unemployed do not care who was right in 1924.

It works, barely. The delegation that goes to Cainholde is small and its instructions are stiff, but it goes, and it does not walk out.

The effect at home is felt within the month. The <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> quietly retire the sharper end of their anti-Marcher line — the social-fascism language, the leaflets naming our ministers as the workers' real enemy — and in the factory districts and the labor exchanges that language had been doing more damage than either party admitted. Our own <span style="color: #c00000;">Left</span> is vindicated and says so; the argument it has been losing for six years is suddenly the argument that was right.

@leftists
title: Let our own Left carry the message.
subtitle: They have the standing with the <span style="color: #700000;">Collectivists</span> that the rest of us do not - if they are strong enough to spend it.
view-if: collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 3)
