unavailable-subtitle: Our <span style="color: #c00000;">Left</span> would have to be the strongest faction in the party for Halsmark to take the approach seriously.
on-arrival: left_strength += 3; left_dissent -= 4 if left_dissent >= 4; center_dissent -= 3 if center_dissent >= 3; collectivist_relation += 8*(1-dissent); commons_marcher += 2; disfavored_marcher += 2

Our Left goes to them, and goes as what it is: the largest faction of the largest workers' party on the continent, which is a thing Halsmark's people cannot dismiss as easily as they dismiss us. The argument they make is not that the Collectivists should trust the Marchers. It is that a party which will not sit in a room with eleven million voters' representatives has decided to be a sect, and that the men making that decision should say so plainly.

The delegation goes to Cainholde. It is not friendly, and it does not walk out.

The effect at home is felt within the month. The <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> quietly retire the sharper end of their anti-Marcher line — the social-fascism language, the leaflets naming our ministers as the workers' real enemy — and in the factory districts and the labor exchanges that language had been doing more damage than either party admitted. Our own <span style="color: #c00000;">Left</span> is vindicated and says so; the argument it has been losing for six years is suddenly the argument that was right.

@nothing
title: Cainholde is not worth spending anything on.
subtitle: Let them decide for themselves.
view-if: collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 3)

We let the question answer itself, and it answers the way these questions have answered since the Second Conference. Two observers are sent, without instructions to speak. They file a report describing the proceedings as a rehearsal for the next betrayal, and it is printed in full.

@blind
title: The <span style="color: #700000;">Collectivists</span> remain blind to the real world.
view-if: collectivist_leader != "Conciliators" and not (collectivist_relation >= 40 or chancellor_party == "I" or chancellor_party == "Royalist" or chancellor_party == "Traditionalist" or chancellor_party == "CSG" or crisis_urgency >= 3)

There is nothing to answer. A party that has decided the crisis proves it was right about everything will read Cainholde as one more proof, and there is no argument available to us that does not confirm the thesis simply by being ours.

We go without them, as we did last time.
